ochemical reduction potentials  denoted by epsilon or e naught prime now  II not as you know from physical  chemistry or physics denotes the  electrochemical potential in standard  dishes however in biochemistry we use  the anak prime notation to denote that  the pH is taken to account and it's not  what you would expect like a hydrogen  ions concentration equals 1 molar but  rather is a pH of 7 the hydrogen ions  country concentration equals 10 to minus  7 so therefore these numbers are  adjusted to correspond to pH 7 the  electrochemical potentials we see in  this table are reduction potentials and  they tell us how easy it is to reduce a  particular species therefore the higher  the number the easier it is to reduce  that particular species and the more  energy the reduction of that species  will generate therefore the electron  transport chain will go from the species  that are hardest to be reduced towards  the species that are easiest to be  reduced therefore the order of the  electron carriers will be from once they  have the lowest reductive potential to  the ones that have the highest reduction  potential so now if we're going to sort  all these electron carriers in order of  their potential we're gonna get the  following order as you see here so the  electrons are going to flow from the  flavin into the coenzyme q and then the  electrons are going to flow from  coenzyme q to cytochrome B and then  cytochrome c 1 c a and sulfate and as  you can see flavin has like a negative  reduction potential it's like the  hardest to be reduced and the next one  is ubiquinone it's like barely positive  and then the highest number is sulfate  0.4 8 volts now let's take a closer look  how the electrons are going to be  transferred through this proposed  electron transport chain in the first  reaction here we have the flavin I've  written the flavin adenine dinucleotide  fadh2 a reduced version that's going to  be converted to the oxidized fa d  version of it and in this redox reaction  we're going to use the coins I'm Q the  oxidized version and reduce it in the  process so the electrons get transferred  from FIDH  to coenzyme q now in the next reaction  the reduced version of coins I'm Q is  going to get oxidized back to coins on Q  and in the process  cytochrome B it's gonna go from the its  oxidized form to its reduced form now  this process continues with every single  step every single Antrim carrier up  until we get to the sulfate where the  reduced form of the cytochrome a will  donate its electrons to the sulfate and  sulfate would get reduced to its reduced  form it's called sulfite alright so if  we were to draw how the electrons move  through this chain so the electrons are  going to start at F ADH  and then they're going to be transferred  to coenzyme q in the reduced form and  then coenzyme q is going to pass it to  the cytochrome b that's going to be in  its reduced form and then cytochrome B  is going to pass it to cytochrome C 1  and then cytochrome C cytochrome a and  finally they're going to end up in  sulfite another thing to notice here is  that except for the initial flavin and  the final electron acceptor sulfate all  the other intermediates get regenerated  so we go from the oxidized version to  the reduced version and back to the  oxidized version so all these electron  carriers are going to be sufficient only  in catalytic amounts so the only thing  that gets consumed is the fadh2 and the  sulfate these are two reactants and we  get in this reaction F ad and sulfite  some would just said will help us segue  into the Part B of the problem which  asked us to calculate how much energy do  we get by converting one molecule of  fadh2 in one molecule of sulfate into F  ad and sulfite respectively no as we  pointed out here only the fadh2 and  sulfate are consumed in this reaction  all the other electron carriers are  recycled and regenerated in in the  course of the electron transport chain  in order to calculate the energy it's  useful first to write the half reaction  of the redox processes here are the two  half reactions of this redox process  fadh2 gets oxidized to F ad and donates  its two electrons and the epsilon or e0  prime is minus zero point 22 volts now  this is the potential from the table and  that's a reduction potential the  equation as written is an oxidation and  therefore the potential that we need to  take into account is the minus of this  one sulfate is then gonna accept the two  electrons and gonna get reduced to the  sulfide and water and the  electrochemical potential for this is  zero point four eight volts so now when  we add these two together we get the the  overall process where fadh2 gets  oxidized by sulfate to generate F ad and  sulfite and the electro-motive force is  just the mathematical sum of these two  keeping in mind that this has to be  taken with negative sign because again  as written this is an oxidation this is  the potential for the reduction reaction  so the electro-motive force it's  actually 0.7 volts now we can easily  convert from the electro-motive force to  a delta g naught prime value and the  relationship is written here Delta G  naught permits  and F Delta e not prime and is the  number of electrons in the process as we  see here to F is the Faraday's constant  and Delta e not prime is going to be the  electro-motive force and if we go  through the number-crunching we get a  delta g naught prime minus 135  kilojoules per mole notice because it's  a negative number that means is a  spontaneous process as written and as  you know a negative Delta G will  correspond to a positive electro-motive  force now we're just one step away from  calculating how much ATP we can produce  with this energy as you know we generate  ATP out of adp and phosphate and this is  the the reaction that's catalyzed by ATP  synthase and it takes about thirty point  five kilo joules per mole to form ATP  out of adp and phosphate therefore the  135 kilojoules per mole that were  generated from one mole of fadh2 it's  going to be enough for about 4 molecules  of ATP s this is in contrast which was  the normal processes that use oxygen as  their final electron acceptor we're out  of one fadh2 molecule we generate at  most two molecule of ATP so in some ways  sulfate is actually a better electron  acceptor and can give us more energy  Part C of this problem deals with a  culture of this microorganism in the lab  and we're adding to this culture by  nitro phenol a compound  we're told has a pKa of about 5.2 so  let's explore what happens to the  electron transport chain of the organism  when we add the nitro phenol  here I put together a cartoon  representation of the electron transport  chain of our organism so as you can see  here this is the extracellular  environment this is the outer membrane  this is the inner membrane where we have  all these complexes I denoted here with  these rectangles of the electron  transport chain and fadh2 for example  it's going to donate its electrons  be passed along all the way to sulfate  and in the process protons are going to  get pumped into this inter membrane  space now these protons can be used in  the ATP synthase as they travel back  into the intracellular space their  energy can be used to convert ADP and  inorganic phosphate to ADP as we just  discussed in part 2 now to this organism  we said we're going to add the nitro  phenol  here's the structure of denied Ruffino  and we're told the pKa of this proton  right here the pKa is about 5.2 when  this compound diffuses through the  membrane is going to go through a to  this inter membrane space so it has a  very low pH and also in a in the  intracellular space on the cytosol which  has a much higher pH right so because  PKA 5.2 it's a relatively low much lower  than 7 PKA in the intermembrane space  where it's more acidic it's gonna be  protonated so we can write for example  that Ruffino  Oh H in equilibrium with a nitro phenol  Oh minus plus a proton okay now because  here we have a lot of protons this this  equilibrium will be shifted to the left  that is the protonated form of denied  rafino  however here in the cytosol the NP o h  it's gonna be in the same equilibrium or  minus plus h plus  but because the ph is fairly high that  is there are not a lot of protons so  this equilibrium is going to be shifted  to the right this equilibrium is gonna  be shifted to the left all right so now  look what happens so because this  equilibrium is shifted to the left it's  gonna keep soaking up a lot of these  protons all right then the neutral die  nitro phenol molecule is gonna diffuse  through the membrane as such and enter  the intracellular space the cytosol  where it's gonna be deprotonated The  Commitments to shift it to the right  so in effect fenetre phenol is gonna  carry the protons from the intermembrane  space in inside the cell now it's going  to do that in parallel with the protons  that we're gonna be flowing through the  ATP synthase to generate ATP so in  effect we're discharging this battery  where the constitution of protons is  basically our  electrochemical gradient is gonna be  discharging the battery without  producing ATP so as you know if your  short circuit the battery the battery is  gonna heat up because you're discharging  an electrochemical gradient similarly by  nitro phenol  by taking these protons from the  internal memory space and bringing them  inside into the intracellular space it's  gonna be generating Heat  therefore we can answer the part C by  saying that the medium in which these  cells are growing is gonna heat up when  we add the natural phenol to it the  process is described in this problem are  fairly universal now in eukaryotes like  more evolved organisms they would happen  in the mitochondrion if you look back at  this diagram if this was the double  membrane of the mitochondrion this would  be the inside of the cell that contains  the mitochondria this would be the inter  membrane space and this would be the  inside of the mitochondria or the  mitochondrial matrix right similarly by  adding a compound like diametral phenol  who can dissipate the electrochemical  gradient in the mitochondria and cause  the cell to heat up in fact this process  is actually used by a number of  organisms to generate heat instead of  chemical energy or ATP for example the  brown fat cells in newborn in mammals  have a special protein that allows to  dissipate this electrochemical gradient  in the mitochondria to generate heat  another good example is the the seeds of  many plants when they germinate they  actually generate a lot of heat that can  be used to melt the ice or the snow  around them that's why some of the  plants can start growing even before the  snow has melt and the early spring I  hope that working through this problem  will help you understand better the  inner workings of an electron transport  chain and how it can convert the  chemical energy of chemical bonds into  an electrochemical gradient which can  then be used to generate high-energy  compounds like ATP or it can be  dissipated to generate heat

